    "summary": "Fire‑starter first‑set trio, soaring jams; second set uneven",
    "blurb": "The Spectrum show bursts open with a blistering In The Midnight Hour → Walkin' The Dog → Big Boss Man sequence and deep Althea. Tons Of Steel, Supplication Jam and the Estimated Prophet → Eyes of the World segment showcase the band's high‑energy peak. The second set wanders, with Revolution and some later numbers feeling sloppy.",
    "review": "April 8 1985 at the Spectrum captured a Grateful Dead in high gear early on, delivering one of the spring‑'85 tour’s most electrifying openings. Jerry’s guitar was “on fire” through the three‑song streak that kicked off the night, and his gritty vocals added a raw edge to Althea. Brent’s keyboards shone on Tons Of Steel and the sprawling Supplication Jam, while Phil laid down solid bass foundations despite occasional mic quirks. The first set flowed like a second set, keeping intensity high throughout.\n\nThe second half is more mixed. Estimated Prophet segued into an inspired Eyes of the World jam that lifted the band back up, but Revolution and later numbers suffered from vocal fatigue and a sense of sloppiness that several listeners noted. Bob’s rhythm guitar was sometimes buried in the mix, yet his bluesy contributions to Midnight Hour and Cumberland Blues remain highlights. Drummers Mickey and Bill kept a tight pulse, even sparking a brief “battle” during the GDTRFB outro. Overall, the show is worth digging for its explosive first‑set moments and standout jams, while accepting that the latter portion drifts.\n\nFor anyone looking to experience this night, the Gills soundboard (gd85-04-08.sbd.gills.20010.sbeok.shnf) offers the clearest, most balanced capture of the band’s highs without overwhelming muddiness.",
